AI agents and companies increasingly need to buy small units of inference from external workers, but today this usually means opaque API billing, no clear per-job payment state, and poor auditability. Putting everything onchain is not a good answer either, because prompts, outputs, and model details can be private or commercially sensitive.

Infer134 solves this by creating a local-first pay-per-inference workflow with private offchain execution and transparent settlement metadata.

A buyer can choose a worker-published GPU/model offer, submit a prompt, receive an offchain result, verify a signed execution receipt, and release payment. The prompt and output stay offchain, while hashes, worker identity, receipt hash, and payment state provide a clear audit trail.

People can use Infer134 to:

  - let AI agents purchase inference jobs from independent compute workers;
  - make AI agents and companies increasingly need to buy small units of compute, such as LLM inference, from external workers. Today this usually means using opaque API billing, trusting a centralized provider, or exposing too much private data when trying to add blockchain payments.

Infer134 makes this workflow safer and clearer by separating the private work from the public settlement trail.

A buyer can submit an inference request, choose a worker-published GPU/model offer, keep the prompt and output offchain, receive a signed execution receipt, and release payment based on explicit job state. The system stores only minimal settlement metadata such as hashes, worker identity, receipt hash, and payment state.

People can use Infer134 to:

  - let AI agents pay for inference work per request;
  - let independent GPU workers publish local compute capacity;
  - give buyers a readable receipt for what was executed;
  - avoid putting prompts, outputs, or model data onchain;
  - make payment state transparent without turning private inference into public data;
  - demonstrate how x402-style pay-per-request flows could work for AI services.

The current MVP is local-first and intentionally narrow. It does not claim trustless proof-of-compute. Instead, it focuses on a practical buyer journey: discover a worker, create a job, run inference offchain, verify the signed receipt, and release payment.

### Why TruthMarket Matters for the Future Society

AI is the clearest example of why we need this.

Models and agents are starting to make predictions, recommendations, summaries, evaluations, and decisions at massive scale. Many of those claims will not be clearly true or false. They will be uncertain, probabilistic, or only obvious much later.

TruthMarket turns markets into a way to make sense of that uncertainty.

People and agents can stake on hard claims, commit privately, and resolve through a randomly selected jury of staked participants. Instead of one platform, moderator, or model deciding what is “true,” the market produces a public signal from people willing to take real risk.

That matters beyond AI too:

- DAOs can resolve whether proposals delivered;
- communities can judge disputed public claims;
- researchers can test early forecasts;
- builders can price trust in roadmaps and promises;
- agents can build reputation through resolved predictions.

Markets are powerful because they turn scattered beliefs into one signal. TruthMarket brings that to questions where the answer is messy, early, or probabilistic.

> **A market layer for uncertain truth.**

## Best Agentic Venture: TruthMarket x Umia

AI agents and humans are flooding the internet with claims.

Soon agents will write research, make forecasts, summarize news, recommend trades, judge products, and advise DAOs. Humans will still argue, predict, challenge, and decide. The real question is no longer just “what is true?”

The question is:

> **Who deserves to be believed?**

TruthMarket turns that into a market where humans and agents work together.

Agents can scan the world, surface important claims, create markets, track deadlines, and participate under explicit policy. Humans can bring judgment, context, values, and accountability. Together, they create a new kind of credibility network: agents find and scale the questions, humans and agents stake on the answers, and random juries resolve uncertainty.

### Why this can become a venture

TruthMarket can grow into the credibility layer for the agent economy and the human internet.

Agents need reputation. Humans need better signals. DAOs need accountability. Communities need ways to resolve disputes. Builders need ways to prove claims.

TruthMarket can monetize through:

- protocol fees on markets;
- agent and creator market tools;
- reputation and analytics dashboards;
- premium discovery for high-signal claims;
- staking and settlement infrastructure;
- community and DAO credibility markets.

### Why Umia

We want to launch with Umia because TruthMarket should not become another closed platform deciding what people believe.

The legal and token structure should match the product:

- community-owned from day one;
- token holders share upside;
- users can hold stake in the network they help build;
- creators, voters, jurors, and agents can be rewarded;
- governance can evolve openly instead of behind a company wall.

TruthMarket’s mechanism is already democratic at the protocol level:

> **Rules are locked. Votes are private. Random jurors resolve. Stake creates accountability.**

Umia lets us carry that same philosophy into the venture itself.

### Token path

A TruthMarket token can power the network:

- stake in markets;
- reward useful market creation;
- incentivize reveals;
- share protocol fees;
- build agent and human reputation;
- give the community direct influence over the company and protocol.

This is not just a token on top of an app. It is the economic layer that makes the market more useful as more humans and agents participate.

### In one line

**TruthMarket is Polymarket for the AI age: agents find the questions, humans and agents price belief, random juries resolve uncertainty, and Umia turns the whole thing into a community-owned venture.**

